.TH SPLIT 1
.SH NAME
split \- split a file into pieces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B split
[
.B \-\fIn
]
[ file [ name ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Split
reads
.I file
and writes
it in
.IR n -line
pieces
(default 1000), as many as necessary,
onto
a set of output files.  The name of the first output
file is
.I name
with
.B aa
appended, and so on
lexicographically.
If no output name is given,
.B x
is default.
.PP
If no input file is given, or
if
.B \-
is given in its stead,
then the standard input file is used.
.PP
.B Note:
the pieces are created in the
.B working directory,
the name being handed to
.IR fopen (3)
with no directory part prepended.
A
.I name
containing a
.B /
therefore writes wherever it points; nothing is created for it, and a missing directory is
reported as
.BR "Cannot create output" .
An existing piece is
.B truncated
without warning.
Both are v7's and its page said neither.
.SH "HOW MANY PIECES"
The suffix is two letters, so
.B 676
pieces can be named:
.B aa
through
.BR zz .
.PP
.B Note:
the 677th is refused, with
.BR "split: more than 676 output files" .
v7 carried on past
.B zz
into
.BR "{a" ,
.BR "{b"
and beyond, with no complaint \- so the promise above that the names run lexicographically
stopped being true exactly when a user had enough pieces to care.
What has already been written is left written; the input has been consumed.
.PP
.B Note:
.I name
may be at most
.B 97
bytes, the buffer being 100 and the suffix and terminator taking three.
A longer one is refused with
.B "split: output name too long"
before anything is created.
v7 copied it in with no test at all.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Note:
.B \-0
is refused with
.BR "split: line count must be positive" .
v7 accepted it and then looped forever, reading nothing and writing nothing: with a count of
zero the per-piece loop runs no iterations, so no input is read, no output is opened, and the
program goes round again.
.PP
.B Note:
an unrecognised option is refused with a usage line.
v7 ignored it silently and split the file into thousand-line pieces the user had not asked
for.
.PP
Every diagnostic above exits 1, as does an input that cannot be opened.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cat(1), tee(1), wc(1)
